        "content": "---\nrp1_run_id: b79a7997-5b41-45bd-8249-668945b4331b\n---\n# Requirements Specification: YAML Validator Command\n\n**Feature ID**: add\n**Version**: 1.0.0\n**Status**: Draft\n**Created**: 2026-04-03\n\n## 1. Feature Overview\n\nThe project needs a command-line capability that allows developers to validate whether a given YAML file contains well-formed YAML syntax. This command must be implemented without introducing any new external dependencies, relying solely on the existing project toolchain and hand-written logic.\n\n## 2. Business Context\n\n### 2.1 Problem Statement\n\nDevelopers working with YAML configuration files currently have no built-in way to quickly check whether a YAML file is syntactically valid within this project's toolset. They must rely on external tools or manually inspect files, which slows down workflows and increases the risk of deploying malformed configuration.\n\n### 2.2 Business Value\n\n- Reduces developer friction when working with YAML-based configuration files.\n- Catches syntax errors early before they cause runtime failures.\n- Keeps the project dependency footprint minimal by not requiring additional packages.\n\n### 2.3 Success Metrics\n\n- Developers can validate any YAML file from the command line in a single invocation.\n- Valid YAML files produce a clear success message and a zero exit code.\n- Invalid YAML files produce a clear, actionable error message and a non-zero exit code.\n- No new entries are added to `package.json` dependencies or devDependencies (beyond what already exists).\n\n## 3. Stakeholders & Users\n\n### 3.1 User Types\n\n| User Type | Description | Relevance |\n|-----------|-------------|-----------|\n| Developer | A developer using this project's CLI tools to manage configurations or validate files. | Primary user of the YAML validator command. |\n\n### 3.2 Stakeholder Interests\n\n| Stakeholder | Interest |\n|-------------|----------|\n| Project maintainers | Keep the dependency tree minimal while expanding utility functionality. |\n| Developers | Have a fast, reliable, zero-setup way to validate YAML syntax. |\n\n## 4. Scope Definition\n\n### 4.1 In Scope\n\n- A CLI command that accepts a file path argument and validates the YAML syntax of that file.\n- Human-readable output indicating whether the file is valid or describing the syntax error encountered.\n- Appropriate process exit codes (0 for valid, non-zero for invalid or error).\n- Validation of basic YAML syntax (correct indentation structure, proper key-value formatting, valid data types, correct use of special characters).\n\n### 4.2 Out of Scope\n\n- YAML schema validation (validating against a specific schema or structure).\n- Support for multiple YAML documents in a single file (multi-document streams).\n- YAML file editing, formatting, or auto-correction.\n- Reading YAML from stdin (file path input only for MVP).\n- GUI or web-based interfaces.\n- Support for YAML tags or custom types.\n\n### 4.3 Assumptions\n\n- The Bun runtime is available and is the execution environment.\n- YAML files to be validated are encoded in UTF-8.\n- The validator targets common YAML syntax patterns (mappings, sequences, scalars, comments, quoted strings, multi-line strings).\n- Users will provide a single file path as input per invocation.\n\n## 5. Functional Requirements\n\n### REQ-001: Accept File Path Input\n- **Priority**: Must Have\n- **User Type**: Developer\n- **Requirement**: The validator command must accept a file path as a command-line argument specifying the YAML file to validate.\n- **Rationale**: Developers need to point the tool at specific files they want to check.\n- **Acceptance Criteria**:\n  - AC-1: The command accepts a file path as a positional argument.\n  - AC-2: If no file path is provided, the command displays a usage message and exits with a non-zero code.\n  - AC-3: If the specified file does not exist, the command displays a \"file not found\" error and exits with a non-zero code.\n\n### REQ-002: Validate YAML Syntax\n- **Priority**: Must Have\n- **User Type**: Developer\n- **Requirement**: The command must parse the content of the specified file and determine whether it contains syntactically valid YAML.\n- **Rationale**: This is the core purpose of the feature -- catching syntax errors before they cause downstream failures.\n- **Acceptance Criteria**:\n  - AC-1: A well-formed YAML file produces a success message (e.g., \"Valid YAML\") and the process exits with code 0.\n  - AC-2: A YAML file with syntax errors produces an error message that includes the nature of the error and the process exits with a non-zero code.\n  - AC-3: An empty file is treated as valid YAML (empty documents are valid per the YAML specification).\n\n### REQ-003: No New Dependencies\n- **Priority**: Must Have\n- **User Type**: Project maintainer\n- **Requirement**: The YAML validation capability must be implemented without adding any new entries to `package.json` dependencies or devDependencies.\n- **Rationale**: The explicit constraint from the feature request is to keep the dependency footprint unchanged.\n- **Acceptance Criteria**:\n  - AC-1: The `dependencies` and `devDependencies` sections of `package.json` remain unchanged after implementation.\n  - AC-2: No new `node_modules` packages are installed as a result of this feature.\n\n### REQ-004: Clear Error Reporting\n- **Priority**: Should Have\n- **User Type**: Developer\n- **Requirement**: When validation fails, the error output should help the developer locate and understand the problem.\n- **Rationale**: An error message that simply says \"invalid\" is not actionable; developers need guidance on what went wrong.\n- **Acceptance Criteria**:\n  - AC-1: Error messages include a description of the syntax issue encountered.\n  - AC-2: Where feasible, error messages reference the approximate location (line number) of the issue.\n\n### REQ-005: Exit Code Behavior\n- **Priority**: Must Have\n- **User Type**: Developer\n- **Requirement**: The command must exit with code 0 on successful validation and a non-zero code on failure (invalid YAML, missing file, or missing arguments).\n- **Rationale**: Proper exit codes enable integration with scripts, CI pipelines, and other tooling.\n- **Acceptance Criteria**:\n  - AC-1: Exit code is 0 when the file contains valid YAML.\n  - AC-2: Exit code is 1 when the file contains invalid YAML.\n  - AC-3: Exit code is 1 when the file path is missing or the file does not exist.\n\n## 6. Non-Functional Requirements\n\n### 6.1 Performance Expectations\n\n- The validator should return a result within 1 second for YAML files up to 1 MB in size under normal conditions.\n\n### 6.2 Security Requirements\n\n- The command must only read the specified file; it must not write to or modify any files.\n- The command must not execute or evaluate any content within the YAML file.\n\n### 6.3 Usability Requirements\n\n- The command must be invokable with a single, intuitive command-line invocation.\n- Output messages must be clear and unambiguous (no jargon beyond standard YAML terminology).\n\n### 6.4 Compliance Requirements\n\n- No specific regulatory compliance requirements apply.\n\n## 7. User Stories\n\n### STORY-001: Validate a YAML Configuration File\n- **As a** developer\n- **I want** to run a command that checks whether my YAML file is syntactically correct\n- **So that** I can catch syntax errors before they cause runtime failures\n\n**Acceptance Scenarios**:\n\n- **GIVEN** a file `config.yaml` containing valid YAML syntax, **WHEN** I run the validate command with `config.yaml` as the argument, **THEN** the output indicates the file is valid and the exit code is 0.\n- **GIVEN** a file `config.yaml` containing a YAML syntax error, **WHEN** I run the validate command with `config.yaml` as the argument, **THEN** the output describes the syntax error and the exit code is non-zero.\n\n### STORY-002: Handle Missing File Gracefully\n- **As a** developer\n- **I want** the validator to clearly report when I provide a path to a non-existent file\n- **So that** I can quickly correct my command rather than seeing a confusing error\n\n**Acceptance Scenarios**:\n\n- **GIVEN** I specify a file path that does not exist, **WHEN** I run the validate command, **THEN** the output includes a \"file not found\" message and the exit code is non-zero.\n\n### STORY-003: Get Usage Help When No Arguments Provided\n- **As a** developer\n- **I want** to see usage instructions when I run the command without arguments\n- **So that** I understand how to use it correctly\n\n**Acceptance Scenarios**:\n\n- **GIVEN** no file path argument is provided, **WHEN** I run the validate command, **THEN** usage instructions are displayed and the exit code is non-zero.\n\n## 8. Business Rules\n\n- BR-001: The validator must not add, remove, or modify any file on disk. It is strictly a read-and-report tool.\n- BR-002: The validator must not introduce any new runtime or development dependencies to the project.\n- BR-003: An empty file is considered valid YAML.\n- BR-004: The validator targets single-document YAML files only.\n\n## 9. Dependencies & Constraints\n\n| Dependency / Constraint | Description |\n|-------------------------|-------------|\n| Bun runtime | The project runs on Bun; the command must be compatible with the Bun runtime environment. |\n| No new dependencies | Explicitly required by the feature request. The implementation must use only built-in capabilities and hand-written logic. |\n| Existing project structure | The command should integrate with the existing `src/` directory structure and be consistent with the current CLI pattern in `src/index.ts`. |\n\n## 10. Clarifications Log\n\nNo interactive clarifications were requested (AFK mode). All ambiguities were resolved via inference. See AFK Mode sections below.\n\n## AFK Mode: Auto-Selected Defaults\n\n| Decision Point | Choice | Rationale |\n|----------------|--------|-----------|\n| PRD association | None | No PRDs found in `.rp1/work/prds/`. Continued without PRD linkage. |\n| Charter association | None | No charter found at `.rp1/context/charter.md`. Continued without charter context. |\n\n## AFK Mode: Inferred Decisions\n\n| Ambiguity | Resolution | Source |\n|-----------|------------|--------|\n| \"simple\" -- scope of validation | Syntax validation only (well-formed YAML), not schema validation | Conservative default; schema validation would be a significantly larger scope |\n| \"command\" -- type of interface | CLI command invokable from the terminal via a file path argument | Consistent with existing CLI pattern in `src/index.ts` (KB: index.md) |\n| Input source not specified | File path as positional argument | Most common pattern for CLI file validators; consistent with existing argv usage in `src/index.ts` |\n| Output format not specified | Human-readable text to stdout with appropriate exit codes | Standard CLI validator convention |\n| User types not specified | Developers using the project's CLI tools | Inferred from project domain (\"Utility scripting\") in concept_map.md |\n| Error detail level not specified | Include error description and approximate line number where feasible | Conservative default balancing usefulness with \"simple\" constraint |\n| Empty file handling not specified | Treat as valid YAML | Per YAML specification, an empty document is valid |\n"
      },
