        "content": "---\nrp1_run_id: b79a7997-5b41-45bd-8249-668945b4331b\n---\n# Technical Design: YAML Validator Command\n\n**Feature ID**: add\n**Version**: 1.0.0\n**Status**: Draft\n**Created**: 2026-04-03\n\n## 1. Design Overview\n\nThis design describes a CLI command that validates YAML file syntax within the existing Bun/TypeScript project. The implementation uses a hand-written YAML syntax validator (no new dependencies) exposed through a dedicated CLI entry point. The design follows the project's existing patterns: a new script file for the CLI entry point, a separate pure-function module for the validation logic, and co-located tests using Bun's built-in test runner.\n\n**Key Design Principles**:\n- Zero new dependencies (hard constraint from requirements)\n- Pure-function validation logic separated from CLI concerns\n- Consistent with existing project patterns (file naming, code style, test approach)\n- Conservative scope: syntax validation only, single-document YAML\n\n```mermaid\ngraph LR\n    CLI[\"validate-yaml.ts<br/>(CLI Entry Point)\"] -->|file path| FS[\"File System<br/>(Bun.file API)\"]\n    FS -->|file content| VAL[\"yaml-validator.ts<br/>(Validation Logic)\"]\n    VAL -->|result| CLI\n    CLI -->|stdout + exit code| USER[\"Developer\"]\n```\n\n## 2. Architecture\n\nThe feature introduces two new source files that follow the existing single-layer architecture pattern. The CLI entry point handles I/O concerns (argument parsing, file reading, output, exit codes) while the validator module contains only pure validation logic.\n\n### Component Architecture\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TB\n    subgraph \"CLI Layer\"\n        VY[\"src/validate-yaml.ts\"]\n    end\n    subgraph \"Logic Layer\"\n        YV[\"src/yaml-validator.ts\"]\n    end\n    subgraph \"Test Layer\"\n        YVT[\"src/yaml-validator.test.ts\"]\n    end\n\n    VY -->|imports validateYaml| YV\n    YVT -->|imports validateYaml| YV\n    VY -->|reads file| BF[\"Bun.file()\"]\n    VY -->|reads args| PA[\"process.argv\"]\n    VY -->|writes output| CO[\"console.log/error\"]\n    VY -->|sets exit code| PE[\"process.exit()\"]\n```\n\n### Execution Flow\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    participant User\n    participant CLI as validate-yaml.ts\n    participant FS as File System\n    participant Val as yaml-validator.ts\n\n    User->>CLI: bun run validate <filepath>\n    CLI->>CLI: Parse process.argv\n    alt No file path provided\n        CLI->>User: Usage message + exit(1)\n    end\n    CLI->>FS: Bun.file(filepath).exists()\n    alt File not found\n        CLI->>User: \"File not found\" + exit(1)\n    end\n    CLI->>FS: Bun.file(filepath).text()\n    FS-->>CLI: file content (string)\n    CLI->>Val: validateYaml(content)\n    Val-->>CLI: ValidationResult\n    alt Valid YAML\n        CLI->>User: \"Valid YAML: <filepath>\" + exit(0)\n    else Invalid YAML\n        CLI->>User: Error details + exit(1)\n    end\n```\n\n## 3. Detailed Design\n\n### 3.1 Module: `src/yaml-validator.ts`\n\nThis module exports a pure function `validateYaml` that accepts a string of YAML content and returns a structured result indicating validity or describing errors.\n\n**Public Interface**:\n\n```typescript\n/** Result of YAML validation. */\nexport interface ValidationResult {\n  valid: boolean;\n  errors: ValidationError[];\n}\n\n/** A single validation error with location and description. */\nexport interface ValidationError {\n  line: number;\n  message: string;\n}\n\n/**\n * Validate the syntax of a YAML string.\n * Returns a result indicating whether the content is valid YAML\n * and any errors found during parsing.\n */\nexport function validateYaml(content: string): ValidationResult;\n```\n\n**Validation Strategy**:\n\nThe validator uses a line-by-line state-machine approach to check YAML syntax. It tracks indentation levels via an explicit stack and validates structural rules at each line.\n\n**Validation checks performed**:\n\n1. **Indentation consistency**: Each line's indentation must use consistent whitespace (spaces only, no tabs). Child elements must be indented further than parents. De-indentation must return to a previously established level.\n\n2. **Mapping syntax** (`key: value`): Keys must be valid YAML scalars. Colon-space separator is required for block mappings. Duplicate keys at the same indentation level within the same mapping are flagged.\n\n3. **Sequence syntax** (`- item`): Dash-space prefix for sequence items. Items within a sequence must be at a consistent indentation level.\n\n4. **Quoted strings**: Matching single (`'`) or double (`\"`) quotes. Proper escape handling within double-quoted strings.\n\n5. **Multi-line strings**: Block scalar indicators (`|`, `>`, `|+`, `|-`, `>+`, `>-`) followed by content lines at a deeper indentation.\n\n6. **Comments**: Lines starting with `#` (after optional whitespace) or inline comments after a space-`#` are valid.\n\n7. **Empty lines**: Treated as valid (whitespace-only lines are ignored).\n\n8. **Flow collections**: Basic validation of inline `{key: value}` and `[item1, item2]` syntax -- matching braces/brackets.\n\n9. **Document markers**: `---` (document start) and `...` (document end) are recognized at the top level.\n\n10. **Special values**: `null`, `true`, `false`, `~`, numeric values are recognized as valid scalars.\n\n**Data Model**:\n\n```typescript\n/** Internal parser state for tracking indentation context. */\ninterface ParserState {\n  /** Stack of indentation levels. */\n  indentStack: number[];\n  /** Current context type at each indent level. */\n  contextStack: Array<\"mapping\" | \"sequence\" | \"scalar\">;\n  /** Whether currently inside a block scalar (| or >). */\n  inBlockScalar: boolean;\n  /** Expected minimum indentation for block scalar content. */\n  blockScalarIndent: number;\n  /** Line number (1-based). */\n  lineNumber: number;\n  /** Collected errors. */\n  errors: ValidationError[];\n}\n```\n\n### 3.2 Module: `src/validate-yaml.ts`\n\nThis is the CLI entry point, responsible for:\n\n1. **Argument parsing**: Read `process.argv[2]` for the file path. If absent, print usage and exit(1).\n2. **File existence check**: Use `Bun.file(path).exists()` to verify the file. If not found, print error and exit(1).\n3. **File reading**: Use `Bun.file(path).text()` to read file contents as UTF-8 string.\n4. **Validation**: Call `validateYaml(content)` from the validator module.\n5. **Output**: Print results to stdout (success) or stderr (errors) and call `process.exit()` with appropriate code.\n\n**Output Format**:\n\n- Success: `Valid YAML: <filepath>`\n- Failure: `Invalid YAML: <filepath>` followed by one line per error: `  Line <N>: <message>`\n- Missing arg: `Usage: bun run validate <filepath>`\n- File not found: `Error: File not found: <filepath>`\n\n### 3.3 Integration with package.json\n\nAdd a new script entry:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"scripts\": {\n    \"validate\": \"bun run src/validate-yaml.ts\"\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThis allows invocation via `bun run validate <filepath>`.\n\n## 4. Technology Stack\n\n| Category | Choice | Rationale |\n|----------|--------|-----------|\n| Runtime | Bun | Existing project runtime |\n| Language | TypeScript (strict) | Existing project language |\n| File I/O | Bun.file() API | Idiomatic Bun file reading |\n| Testing | Bun built-in test runner | Existing test infrastructure |\n| CLI args | process.argv | Existing pattern from src/index.ts |\n| Dependencies | None new | Hard requirement (REQ-003) |\n\n## 5. Implementation Plan\n\n| # | Component | Description | Files |\n|---|-----------|-------------|-------|\n| T1 | YAML Validator Module | Core validation logic: line-by-line parser with indentation tracking, syntax checks for mappings, sequences, scalars, quoted strings, multi-line blocks, flow collections, and comments | `src/yaml-validator.ts` |\n| T2 | CLI Entry Point | Argument parsing, file reading, output formatting, exit code handling | `src/validate-yaml.ts` |\n| T3 | Validator Tests | Test suite for yaml-validator.ts covering valid YAML, invalid YAML, edge cases | `src/yaml-validator.test.ts` |\n| T4 | Package Script | Add \"validate\" script to package.json | `package.json` |\n\n## 6. Implementation DAG\n\n**Parallel Groups** (tasks with no inter-dependencies):\n\n1. [T1] - Foundation module with no dependencies on other new components\n2. [T2, T3, T4] - All depend on T1's exported interface, but are independent of each other\n\n**Dependencies**:\n\n- T2 -> T1 (Interface: CLI entry point imports `validateYaml` function and types from the validator module)\n- T3 -> T1 (Interface: Tests import `validateYaml` function and `ValidationResult` type from the validator module)\n- T4 -> T2 (Build: Package script references `src/validate-yaml.ts` as the entry point)\n\n**Critical Path**: T1 -> T2 -> T4\n\n## 7. Testing Strategy\n\n### Test Value Assessment\n\n| Test Category | Value | Rationale |\n|---------------|-------|-----------|\n| Valid YAML parsing (various structures) | HIGH | Core business logic -- must correctly accept well-formed YAML |\n| Invalid YAML detection (syntax errors) | HIGH | Core business logic -- must correctly reject malformed YAML |\n| Error message quality (line numbers, descriptions) | MEDIUM | Usability requirement (REQ-004) |\n| Empty file handling | MEDIUM | Specific business rule (BR-003) |\n| CLI argument handling | LOW | Simple process.argv check, minimal logic to test |\n| File existence check | LOW | Thin wrapper around Bun.file().exists(), testing Bun behavior |\n\n### Test Plan\n\nTests are co-located in `src/yaml-validator.test.ts`, targeting the `validateYaml` pure function.\n\n**Valid YAML cases** (expect `{ valid: true, errors: [] }`):\n- Simple key-value mapping (`key: value`)\n- Nested mappings with proper indentation\n- Sequences (lists with `- ` prefix)\n- Mixed mappings and sequences\n- Quoted strings (single and double)\n- Multi-line block scalars (`|` and `>`)\n- Comments (full-line and inline)\n- Empty content\n- Document markers (`---`)\n- Flow collections (`{key: val}`, `[a, b]`)\n- Various scalar types (numbers, booleans, null)\n\n**Invalid YAML cases** (expect `{ valid: false, errors: [...] }`):\n- Tab indentation\n- Inconsistent indentation (de-indent to non-established level)\n- Missing colon-space separator in mapping\n- Unmatched quotes\n- Unmatched braces/brackets in flow collections\n- Invalid indentation within block scalar\n\n**Error reporting cases**:\n- Error messages include approximate line numbers\n- Error messages include descriptive text\n\n**Tests NOT included** (low value):\n- Testing `Bun.file()` behavior (library verification)\n- Testing `process.argv` parsing (framework behavior)\n- Testing `process.exit()` behavior (runtime behavior)\n- E2E CLI invocation tests (integration testing beyond unit scope)\n\n## 8. Deployment Design\n\nNo special deployment steps required. The feature is a new CLI command within the existing project structure.\n\n**Integration steps**:\n1. New files are added to `src/` directory (already included in `tsconfig.json` via `\"include\": [\"src/**/*\"]`).\n2. New `validate` script added to `package.json`.\n3. No build step required (Bun runs TypeScript directly).\n4. No environment variables or configuration needed.\n\n**Verification**: Run `bun run validate <test-file.yaml>` to confirm the command is operational after implementation.\n\n## 9. Documentation Impact\n\n| Type | Target | Section | KB Source | Rationale |\n|------|--------|---------|-----------|-----------|\n| edit | `.rp1/context/index.md` | Project Structure | index.md:Project Structure | Add new files to the structure listing |\n| edit | `.rp1/context/architecture.md` | Layers | architecture.md:Layers | Add validate-yaml.ts to Entry layer and yaml-validator.ts to Utils layer |\n| edit | `.rp1/context/architecture.md` | Scripts | architecture.md:Scripts | Add `bun run validate` script |\n| edit | `.rp1/context/modules.md` | (module listing) | modules.md | Add entries for new modules |\n\n## 10. Design Decisions Log\n\n| ID | Decision | Choice | Rationale | Alternatives Considered |\n|----|----------|--------|-----------|------------------------|\n| D1 | CLI entry point structure | Separate file `src/validate-yaml.ts` | Follows existing pattern of `src/index.ts` as a standalone script entry point; keeps concerns separated | Adding a subcommand to `src/index.ts` (rejected: changes existing behavior, mixes concerns) |\n| D2 | Validator module structure | Separate pure-function module `src/yaml-validator.ts` | Follows the utils pattern of exporting pure functions; enables isolated unit testing | Inline validation in CLI file (rejected: not testable in isolation, violates separation of concerns) |\n| D3 | YAML parsing approach | Line-by-line state machine with indentation stack | Handles YAML's indentation-based structure naturally; straightforward to implement incrementally | Recursive descent parser (rejected: more complex for indentation-sensitive grammar); regex-only approach (rejected: insufficient for nested structures) |\n| D4 | File reading API | Bun.file() API | Idiomatic Bun approach; async-friendly; aligns with project runtime | Node.js fs module (rejected: not idiomatic for Bun project, though would work) |\n| D5 | Error reporting format | Text to stderr with line numbers | Matches CLI conventions; actionable for developers; satisfies REQ-004 | JSON output (rejected: over-engineered for CLI use); single error message without line info (rejected: insufficient per REQ-004) |\n| D6 | Test co-location | `src/yaml-validator.test.ts` co-located with source | Matches existing pattern from `src/utils.test.ts` | Separate `tests/` directory (rejected: breaks existing pattern) |\n| D7 | Package script name | `validate` | Concise, descriptive, does not conflict with existing scripts | `validate-yaml` (acceptable but longer); `check-yaml` (acceptable alternative) |\n"
