        "file_path": "/private/tmp/rp1-evals/495896a9/workspace/.rp1/work/features/add/hypotheses.md",
        "content": "# Hypothesis Document: add\n**Version**: 1.0.0 | **Created**: 2026-04-03 | **Status**: VALIDATED\n\n## Hypotheses\n\n### HYP-001: Bun.file() API supports exists() and text() methods\n**Risk Level**: HIGH\n**Status**: CONFIRMED\n**Statement**: The design assumes Bun.file(path) returns an object with an `exists()` method (returning a Promise<boolean>) and a `text()` method (returning a Promise<string>) for reading file contents as UTF-8.\n**Context**: The CLI entry point (src/validate-yaml.ts) depends entirely on these APIs for file existence checking and content reading. If unavailable, the implementation approach must change.\n**Validation Criteria**:\n- CONFIRM if: `Bun.file(path).exists()` returns a Promise<boolean> and `Bun.file(path).text()` returns a Promise<string> with file content\n- REJECT if: These methods do not exist or behave differently than expected\n**Suggested Method**: CODE_EXPERIMENT\n\n### HYP-002: process.argv is available and works in Bun for CLI argument parsing\n**Risk Level**: MEDIUM\n**Status**: CONFIRMED\n**Statement**: The design assumes process.argv is available in Bun and follows the Node.js convention where argv[0] is the runtime, argv[1] is the script, and argv[2]+ are user arguments.\n**Context**: The CLI entry point uses process.argv[2] to read the file path argument. The existing src/index.ts already uses this pattern, but it should be confirmed.\n**Validation Criteria**:\n- CONFIRM if: process.argv[2] correctly receives the first user-provided argument when running `bun run src/file.ts arg`\n- REJECT if: process.argv is not available or argument positions differ\n**Suggested Method**: CODEBASE_ANALYSIS\n\n### HYP-003: process.exit() works correctly in Bun for setting exit codes\n**Risk Level**: MEDIUM\n**Status**: CONFIRMED\n**Statement**: The design assumes process.exit(code) terminates the Bun process with the specified exit code, matching Node.js behavior.\n**Context**: REQ-005 requires exit code 0 for valid YAML and exit code 1 for errors. The implementation calls process.exit() explicitly.\n**Validation Criteria**:\n- CONFIRM if: process.exit(0) and process.exit(1) produce the correct exit codes in Bun\n- REJECT if: process.exit() is unavailable or exit codes are not propagated correctly\n**Suggested Method**: CODE_EXPERIMENT\n\n### HYP-004: Bun can run TypeScript files directly via package.json scripts\n**Risk Level**: LOW\n**Status**: CONFIRMED\n**Statement**: The design assumes `bun run src/validate-yaml.ts` works as a package.json script entry, with Bun executing TypeScript directly without a compilation step.\n**Context**: The package.json will have a \"validate\" script set to \"bun run src/validate-yaml.ts\". The existing \"dev\" script follows this pattern.\n**Validation Criteria**:\n- CONFIRM if: Existing `bun run dev` (which runs `bun run src/index.ts`) executes TypeScript directly without errors\n- REJECT if: Bun requires a build step or cannot run .ts files directly\n**Suggested Method**: CODEBASE_ANALYSIS\n\n### HYP-005: A hand-written line-by-line YAML parser is feasible for basic syntax validation\n**Risk Level**: HIGH\n**Status**: CONFIRMED\n**Statement**: The design proposes a line-by-line state machine parser that can validate basic YAML syntax (indentation, mappings, sequences, quoted strings, block scalars, flow collections, comments) without using any YAML parsing library.\n**Context**: This is the core technical approach for the entire feature. If a hand-written parser cannot reliably handle these YAML constructs, the implementation will be significantly more complex or may need a different approach. The no-dependency constraint (REQ-003) makes this the only viable path.\n**Validation Criteria**:\n- CONFIRM if: A state-machine approach can correctly identify valid/invalid YAML for the specified syntax subset (indentation, mappings, sequences, quoted strings, block scalars, flow collections)\n- REJECT if: The YAML syntax subset is too complex for a line-by-line approach and requires a full recursive descent parser or other fundamentally different technique\n**Suggested Method**: EXTERNAL_RESEARCH\n\n### HYP-006: Bun's test runner can discover and run co-located .test.ts files\n**Risk Level**: LOW\n**Status**: CONFIRMED\n**Statement**: The design assumes `bun test` will automatically discover and run `src/yaml-validator.test.ts` alongside the existing `src/utils.test.ts`.\n**Context**: The testing strategy relies on Bun's test runner finding the new test file. The existing project already uses this pattern.\n**Validation Criteria**:\n- CONFIRM if: `bun test` discovers and runs all `*.test.ts` files in the src/ directory\n- REJECT if: Bun test runner requires explicit configuration to find additional test files\n**Suggested Method**: CODE_EXPERIMENT\n\n## Validation Findings\n\n### HYP-001 Findings\n**Validated**: 2026-04-03T00:00:00Z\n**Method**: CODE_EXPERIMENT\n**Result**: CONFIRMED\n\n**Evidence**:\nA disposable experiment script was written and executed using Bun v1.3.3. The experiment:\n1. Created a test file using `Bun.write()`, then called `Bun.file(path)` on it.\n2. `Bun.file()` returns an object (Blob constructor).\n3. `exists()` returns a Promise that resolves to `true` (boolean) for an existing file.\n4. `text()` returns a Promise that resolves to the file's UTF-8 string content (\"hello world\").\n5. For a non-existent file path, `exists()` resolves to `false`.\n\nAll assertions passed. Output:\n```\ntypeof Bun.file(): object\nexists() is Promise: true\nexists() resolved value: true\nexists() resolved type: boolean\ntext() is Promise: true\ntext() resolved value: \"hello world\"\ntext() resolved type: string\nnon-existent exists(): false\nALL CHECKS PASSED: true\n```\n\n**Sources**:\n- Experimental code: `/tmp/hypothesis-add/hyp001-bun-file-api.ts` (DISPOSABLE)\n\n**Implications for Design**:\nThe design's use of `Bun.file(path).exists()` and `Bun.file(path).text()` is fully supported. No changes needed.\n\n---\n\n### HYP-002 Findings\n**Validated**: 2026-04-03T00:00:00Z\n**Method**: CODEBASE_ANALYSIS\n**Result**: CONFIRMED\n\n**Evidence**:\nThe existing codebase already uses `process.argv[2]` in `src/index.ts:7`:\n```typescript\nconst name = process.argv[2] ?? \"World\";\n```\nThe `package.json` \"dev\" script is `\"bun run src/index.ts\"`, and the project functions correctly with this pattern. The `??` nullish coalescing operator confirms that `process.argv[2]` is `undefined` when no argument is passed (matching Node.js convention where argv[0]=runtime, argv[1]=script, argv[2]+=user args).\n\nAdditionally, the HYP-003 experiment used `process.argv[2]` to receive an exit code argument, which worked correctly (received \"0\" and \"1\" as expected).\n\n**Sources**:\n- `src/index.ts:7` - existing `process.argv[2]` usage\n- `package.json:8` - \"dev\" script using `bun run src/index.ts`\n\n**Implications for Design**:\nThe design's reliance on `process.argv[2]` for the file path argument is consistent with existing project patterns and confirmed working.\n\n---\n\n### HYP-003 Findings\n**Validated**: 2026-04-03T00:00:00Z\n**Method**: CODE_EXPERIMENT\n**Result**: CONFIRMED\n\n**Evidence**:\nA disposable experiment script tested `process.exit()` with different exit codes:\n\n1. `typeof process.exit` is `\"function\"` -- confirmed available.\n2. Running with argument `0`: process exited, shell captured exit code `0`.\n3. Running with argument `1`: process exited, shell captured exit code `1`.\n\nOutput:\n```\ntypeof process.exit: function\nprocess.exit is function: true\nExiting with code: 0\nEXIT_CODE_0: 0\ntypeof process.exit: function\nprocess.exit is function: true\nExiting with code: 1\nEXIT_CODE_1: 1\n```\n\n**Sources**:\n- Experimental code: `/tmp/hypothesis-add/hyp003-process-exit.ts` (DISPOSABLE)\n\n**Implications for Design**:\nExit code handling via `process.exit(0)` and `process.exit(1)` works exactly as designed. REQ-005 can be implemented as planned.\n\n---\n\n### HYP-004 Findings\n**Validated**: 2026-04-03T00:00:00Z\n**Method**: CODEBASE_ANALYSIS\n**Result**: CONFIRMED\n\n**Evidence**:\nThe existing `package.json` already contains a script that runs TypeScript directly:\n```json\n\"dev\": \"bun run src/index.ts\"\n```\nThe file `src/index.ts` is a TypeScript file (contains typed imports and `??` operator). There is no build step, no `tsconfig.json` compilation output, and no `dist/` directory. The project patterns document (`.rp1/context/patterns.md`) confirms: \"No external dependencies beyond Bun built-ins\" and the architecture document confirms `bun run dev` as a working script.\n\nThe HYP-001 and HYP-003 experiments also confirmed that Bun executes `.ts` files directly without compilation (the experiment files were `.ts` and ran without error).\n\n**Sources**:\n- `package.json:8` - \"dev\" script runs `.ts` directly\n- `.rp1/context/patterns.md` - confirms ESM modules with no build step\n- `.rp1/context/architecture.md` - lists `bun run dev` as a working script\n\n**Implications for Design**:\nAdding a `\"validate\": \"bun run src/validate-yaml.ts\"` script to package.json will work without any build configuration.\n\n---\n\n### HYP-005 Findings\n**Validated**: 2026-04-03T00:00:00Z\n**Method**: EXTERNAL_RESEARCH\n**Result**: CONFIRMED\n\n**Evidence**:\nResearch into the YAML 1.2.2 specification and YAML parsing literature reveals significant nuance:\n\n**Full YAML parsing is extremely complex.** The YAML 1.2.2 specification defines 85+ numbered productions (some sources count up to 211 grammar rules when including parameterized variants). It uses \"Parameterized BNF\" format, and creating a fully compliant parser is acknowledged as very difficult. The specification itself notes structure is determined by indentation requiring multi-line context awareness.\n\n**However, the hypothesis asks about a *subset*, not full compliance.** The key distinction is:\n- A **full YAML parser** (handling anchors/aliases, all tag types, arbitrary nesting, flow styles with unlimited lookahead) is NOT feasible with a simple line-by-line approach.\n- A **basic syntax validator** for the specified subset (indentation consistency, key: value mappings, `- ` sequences, quoted strings, `|`/`>` block scalar indicators, `[]`/`{}` flow collections, `#` comments) IS feasible with a line-by-line state machine.\n\nThe feasibility of the subset approach is supported by:\n1. **Indentation validation** is inherently line-by-line: check that each line's leading spaces are consistent and properly nested (track an indentation stack).\n2. **Mapping syntax** (`key: value`) can be detected per-line with pattern matching.\n3. **Sequence items** (`- value`) are line-level constructs.\n4. **Quoted strings** can be validated by tracking open/close quotes within a line (with state carried across lines for multi-line quoted strings).\n5. **Block scalar indicators** (`|`, `>`) set a state that the following lines are content (validated by indentation level).\n6. **Flow collections** require bracket matching state across lines but are tractable.\n7. **Comments** (`#`) are straightforward to detect.\n\nThe approach will NOT catch all invalid YAML (e.g., duplicate keys, type resolution errors, anchor/alias issues), but can reliably catch the most common syntax errors that developers encounter. Projects like StrictYAML demonstrate that validating a restricted YAML subset is a proven approach.\n\n**Sources**:\n- https://yaml.org/spec/1.2.2/ - YAML 1.2.2 Specification (Authoritative): 85+ productions, parameterized BNF grammar\n- https://ktbarrett.github.io/2022/01/08/parsing-yaml.html - \"Complexity in YAML\" (Semi-authoritative): details parsing challenges, plain scalar ambiguity\n- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/YAML - YAML Wikipedia (Semi-authoritative): overview of data structures and indentation rules\n- https://github.com/yaml/yaml-grammar - YAML Grammar project (Authoritative): formal grammar with 211 rules\n- https://github.com/crdoconnor/strictyaml - StrictYAML (Semi-authoritative): demonstrates restricted-subset YAML validation as a viable approach\n\n**Implications for Design**:\nThe line-by-line state machine approach is feasible for the specified subset but the design should:\n1. Clearly document that this is NOT a full YAML parser and will not catch all possible YAML errors.\n2. Focus on the most common syntax errors (bad indentation, missing colons, unclosed quotes/brackets).\n3. Handle block scalar state tracking carefully (indent level after `|` or `>`).\n4. Accept that some edge cases in plain scalars and complex nesting may not be caught.\n5. Consider that multi-line quoted strings and flow collections require cross-line state, which adds complexity but remains feasible within a state machine.\n\n---\n\n### HYP-006 Findings\n**Validated**: 2026-04-03T00:00:00Z\n**Method**: CODE_EXPERIMENT\n**Result**: CONFIRMED\n\n**Evidence**:\nTwo experiments were conducted:\n\n1. **Standalone test file discovery**: A new `.test.ts` file was placed in `/tmp/hypothesis-add/` and `bun test` was run from that directory. Result: `1 pass, 0 fail. Ran 1 test across 1 file.` -- Bun automatically discovered and ran the `.test.ts` file.\n\n2. **Existing project tests**: Running `bun test` from the project root discovered and ran the existing `src/utils.test.ts` file. Result: `2 pass, 0 fail. Ran 2 tests across 1 file.` -- confirming the pattern works.\n\nBun's test runner uses glob patterns to automatically discover `*.test.ts` files without explicit configuration.\n\n**Sources**:\n- Experimental code: `/tmp/hypothesis-add/hyp006-test-discovery.test.ts` (DISPOSABLE)\n- Existing project: `src/utils.test.ts` (confirmed discovery)\n\n**Implications for Design**:\nAdding `src/yaml-validator.test.ts` will be automatically discovered by `bun test` alongside the existing `src/utils.test.ts`. No configuration changes needed.\n\n---\n\n## Summary\n| Hypothesis | Risk | Result | Implication |\n|------------|------|--------|-------------|\n| HYP-001 | HIGH | CONFIRMED | Bun.file() API works exactly as designed with exists() and text() methods |\n| HYP-002 | MEDIUM | CONFIRMED | process.argv follows Node.js convention; existing codebase already uses this pattern |\n| HYP-003 | MEDIUM | CONFIRMED | process.exit() correctly propagates exit codes 0 and 1 |\n| HYP-004 | LOW | CONFIRMED | Bun runs .ts files directly; no build step needed for new script entry |\n| HYP-005 | HIGH | CONFIRMED | Line-by-line state machine feasible for specified YAML subset; not a full parser |\n| HYP-006 | LOW | CONFIRMED | Bun test runner auto-discovers .test.ts files without configuration |\n"
